# Lines of code

https://github.com/code-423n4/2024-10-ramses-exchange/blob/main/contracts/CL/core/RamsesV3Factory.sol#L65-L68


# Vulnerability details

``` 
   function initialize(address _ramsesV3PoolDeployer) external restricted {
@>      require(ramsesV3PoolDeployer == address(0));
        ramsesV3PoolDeployer = _ramsesV3PoolDeployer;
    }
``
## Description:
The `initialize` function lacks proper validation for the `ramsesV3PoolDeployer` argument, allowing it to be called multiple times. This absence of a one-time initialization check means that the `deployer` address can be reset or overwritten, creating an opening for a malicious actor. An attacker could exploit this by calling the function repeatedly with a different address, effectively hijacking control over pool deployment.

## Impact:
This vulnerability can allow an attacker to deploy unauthorized and potentially malicious pools. Such a breach compromises the overall security and integrity of the system, leading to severe consequences such as the creation of pools with malicious intent, which can affect users and the system as a whole.

## Proof of Concept:
If an attacker manages to repeatedly call the `initialize` function with a different `ramsesV3PoolDeployer` address, they can continuously overwrite the `deployer` and take control of the pool deployment process, enabling them to create pools without authorization.

```solidity
//POC Example
function initialize(address newDeployer) external {
    require(ramsesV3PoolDeployer == address(0), "Deployer already set");
    ramsesV3PoolDeployer = newDeployer;
}
// No mechanism here prevents repeated calls and resetting of the deployer
```

## Tools Used:
Manual review

## Recommended Mitigation:
Implement an explicit validation check to ensure that the initialize function can only be called once. This can be done by introducing a `bool` flag that is set to true once the `deployer` is initialized, ensuring subsequent calls are blocked.

``solidity
bool isInitialized = false;

function initialize(address newDeployer) external {
    require(!isInitialized, "Already initialized");
    require(ramsesV3PoolDeployer == address(0), "Deployer already set");
    ramsesV3PoolDeployer = newDeployer;
    isInitialized = true;
}
```



## Assessed type

Access Control

# Lines of code

https://github.com/code-423n4/2024-10-ramses-exchange/blob/main/contracts/CL/core/RamsesV3Factory.sol#L149-L161


# Vulnerability details

```diff
function setPoolFeeProtocolBatch(address[] calldata pools, uint8[] calldata _feeProtocols) external restricted {
        /// @dev AL = Array Length
@>        require(pools.length == _feeProtocols.length, 'AL');
@>        for (uint i; i < pools.length; i++) {
            /// @dev fee cannot exceed 100%
            require(_feeProtocols[i] <= 100, FTL());
            uint8 feeProtocolOld = poolFeeProtocol(pools[i]);
            _poolFeeProtocol[pools[i]] = _feeProtocols[i];
            emit SetPoolFeeProtocol(pools[i], feeProtocolOld, _feeProtocols[i] == 0 ? feeProtocol : _feeProtocols[i]);

            IRamsesV3Pool(pools[i]).setFeeProtocol();
        }
    }
```
## Description:
In the `setPoolFeeProtocolBatch` functions, there are no checks to prevent empty arrays from being passed as arguments. This lack of input validation could result in the function executing unnecessarily when empty arrays are provided, leading to wasted gas and potential logic issues in batch operations.

## Impact:
Passing empty arrays as arguments (e.g., `[]`) would still cause the function to execute, consuming gas without performing any meaningful operations. This could result in higher costs for users and inefficient use of network resources. Additionally, empty input might lead to unintended behavior, as the loop still processes without any actual data, which could introduce subtle logic errors.

## Proof of Concept:
By passing empty arrays to the function, gas is consumed even though no valid operations occur:

```solidity
// Example POC
setPoolFeeProtocolBatch([], []); 
// Function will execute and consume gas, despite no pools or protocols being updated.
```

## Tools Used:
Manual review

## Recommended Mitigation:
Add validation checks to ensure that neither pools nor `_feeProtocols ` arrays are empty before entering the loop. This can prevent unnecessary execution and gas consumption.

```diff
function setPoolFeeProtocolBatch(address[] calldata pools, uint256[] calldata _feeProtocols) external {
    require(pools.length == _feeProtocols.length, 'AL');
++    require(pools.length > 0, "Empty arrays are not allowed");  // New check
    for (uint i; i < pools.length; i++) {
        // processing the logic
    }
}
```
This validation ensures that batch functions are only executed with valid inputs, saving gas and avoiding unexpected behaviors.


## Assessed type

Invalid Validation

# Lines of code

https://github.com/code-423n4/2024-10-ramses-exchange/blob/236e9e9e0cf452828ab82620b6c36c1e6c7bb441/contracts/CL/core/RamsesV3Factory.sol#L106-L116


# Vulnerability details

## Impact
There is a problem that the function [enableTickSpacing](https://github.com/code-423n4/2024-10-ramses-exchange/blob/236e9e9e0cf452828ab82620b6c36c1e6c7bb441/contracts/CL/core/RamsesV3Factory.sol#L106-L116) would fail even when a particular valid tickspacing parameter is passed to the function. 

From the comment in the code which describes the conditions that the function must hold, it says
```solidity
/// @dev tick spacing is capped at 16384 to prevent the situation where tickSpacing is so large that
        /// @dev TickBitmap#nextInitializedTickWithinOneWord overflows int24 container from a valid tick
```
This implies that the tickspacing should be less than or equal to 16384. Hence, when a user sets his/her tickspacing to 16384, it should work. However, it doesn't work from the current implementation of the code as seen below;

```solidity
require(tickSpacing > 0 && tickSpacing < 16384, 'TS');
```
This is because the check is inadequate. This results in Denial of Service for valid tickspacing due to inadequate check.

## Proof of Concept
1. Call enableTickSpacing function
2. Pass 16384 as the tickspacing parameter
3. The require condition is triggered and fails due to inadequate check
```solidity
        require(tickSpacing > 0 && tickSpacing < 16384, 'TS');

```

```solidity
 function enableTickSpacing(int24 tickSpacing, uint24 initialFee) external override restricted {
        require(initialFee < 1_000_000, FTL());
        /// @dev tick spacing is capped at 16384 to prevent the situation where tickSpacing is so large that
        /// @dev TickBitmap#nextInitializedTickWithinOneWord overflows int24 container from a valid tick
        /// @dev 16384 ticks represents a >5x price change with ticks of 1 bips
        require(tickSpacing > 0 && tickSpacing < 16384, 'TS');
        require(tickSpacingInitialFee[tickSpacing] == 0, 'TS!0');

        tickSpacingInitialFee[tickSpacing] = initialFee;
        emit TickSpacingEnabled(tickSpacing, initialFee);
    }
```

## Tools Used
Manual Analysis, Vscode.

## Recommended Mitigation Steps
Change this require condition,
```solidity
        require(tickSpacing > 0 && tickSpacing < 16384, 'TS');
```

to,
```solidity
        require(tickSpacing > 0 && tickSpacing <= 16384, 'TS');

```


## Assessed type

DoS

# Lines of code

https://github.com/code-423n4/2024-10-ramses-exchange/blob/4a40eba36bc47eba8179d4f6203a4b84561a4415/contracts/CL/periphery/SwapRouter.sol#L53-L72


# Vulnerability details

## Proof of Concept
Untrusted External Calls: The contract interacts with external contracts (like pay or the pool) without sufficient validation of the caller.
The pay function is called with parameters derived from the external _data input. If this input is controlled by a malicious actor, they can manipulate the behavior of the contract.
The function can call other contracts or execute methods that may not have been designed with the correct security assumptions, leading to potential vulnerabilities.
Proof of Concept:
Here's how a proof of concept might look for exploiting this vulnerability:

1. Malicious Contract Setup: A malicious contract could be created that calls uniswapV3SwapCallback and manipulates the amount0Delta or amount1Delta values to execute additional swaps or token transfers.
2. Execution:The attacker could exploit the callback to manipulate funds or trigger additional swaps in a manner that the original contract does not expect or allow, potentially draining tokens.

contract MaliciousContract {
    ISwapRouter public router;

    constructor(address _router) {
        router = ISwapRouter(_router);
    }

    function attack(uint256 amountIn) external {
        // Initiate a swap or interaction that triggers the callback
        router.swapExactInput(...);
    }

    function uniswapV3SwapCallback(int256 amount0Delta, int256 amount1Delta, bytes calldata _data) external {
        // Call back into the router and manipulate state or funds
        // This is where the exploit would occur
    }
}


Impact: Potential manipulation of contract logic or unintended fund transfers

Affected Code Example:
/// @inheritdoc IUniswapV3SwapCallback
function uniswapV3SwapCallback(int256 amount0Delta, int256 amount1Delta, bytes calldata _data) external override {
    require(amount0Delta > 0 || amount1Delta > 0); // Ensure that at least one amount is non-zero
    SwapCallbackData memory data = abi.decode(_data, (SwapCallbackData));
    (address tokenIn, address tokenOut, int24 tickSpacing) = data.path.decodeFirstPool();
    CallbackValidation.verifyCallback(deployer, tokenIn, tokenOut, tickSpacing); // Verify that the callback is valid

    (bool isExactInput, uint256 amountToPay) = amount0Delta > 0
        ? (tokenIn < tokenOut, uint256(amount0Delta))
        : (tokenOut < tokenIn, uint256(amount1Delta));
    if (isExactInput) {
        pay(tokenIn, data.payer, msg.sender, amountToPay); // External call to pay
    } else {
        // Initiate next swap or pay
        if (data.path.hasMultiplePools()) {
            data.path = data.path.skipToken();
            exactOutputInternal(amountToPay, msg.sender, 0, data);
        } else {
            amountInCached = amountToPay;
            tokenIn = tokenOut; // Reverse the swap
            pay(tokenIn, data.payer, msg.sender, amountToPay); // Another external call to pay
        }
